    <exercise xml:id="ex-video-flow-visualisation">
      <title>Flow visualisation</title>

      <introduction>
        <aside>
          <title>Optional</title>
          <p>This question is optional. It is meant only to deepen your understanding of the
            processes discussed in this chapter.</p>
        </aside>

        <p>
          <url href="https://techtv.mit.edu/collections/ifluids/videos/32598-flow-visualization">
          Watch the first 13 and half minutes of the video "Flow visualisation" from the NCFMF
          archives</url>.</p>
      </introduction>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Name a few ways in which a fluid flow can be visualised (i.e. how does one produce, in
            an experiment, such a visualisation?)
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p> Define, in words, what <em>pathline</em>, <em>streakline</em>, <em>timeline</em>, and <em>
            streamline</em> means. At 6:30, the author comments that "there is no way to make a
            streamline visible". Discuss this point. </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Give the example, shown in the video, where the pathline, streakline, and streamlines
            all coincide. Draw pictures to illustrate the concept.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Give an example, shown in the video, where the pathline, streakline, and streamlines do
            not coincide. Draw pictures to illustrate the concept.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p> In the video, an intuitive explanation is given for what it means for the velocity to
            be <em>incompressible</em>. What is this explanation? (near 8:25) </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-material-derivative">
      <title>Derivation of the material derivative</title>

      <statement>
        <p> Prove the Eulerian representation of the material derivative, as presented in <xref
            ref="thm-material-derivative" /> by manually expanding the components of the function.
          That is, consider a scalar function <m>f(\bx, t)</m>. Let the spatial coordinate follow <m>\bx
          = \bx(\bX,t)</m> and hence is defined by a specified material label <m>\bX</m>. Then
          derive the identity for <me>
            \DD{f}{t} = \pd{f}{t} \biggr\rvert_{\bX},
          </me> according to <xref
            ref="def-eulerlag" />. </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p> This is the chain rule. Denote the three components of <m>\bx = (x_1, x_2, x_3)</m>.
          Then we differentiate through both outer arguments of <m>f</m>: <md>
            <mrow>\pd{f(\bx(\bX, t), t)}{t}\biggr\rvert_{\bX} &amp;= \pd{f}{t}\biggr\rvert_{\bx} +
          \sum_{i=1}^3 \pd{x_i(\bX, t)}{t}\biggr\rvert_{\bX} \pd{f}{x_i} </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>

        <p> The factor, <me>
            \pd{x_i}{t}\biggr\rvert_{\bX} = \dd{x_i}{t} \equiv u_i,
          </me>
          corresponds to the velocity component in each direction. Then <me>\pd{f(\bx(\bX, t),
          t)}{t}\biggr\rvert_{\bX} = \pd{f}{t} + \bu \cdot \nabla f,
          </me>
          as desired. </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-eulerian-lagrangian">
      <title>Eulerian and Lagrangian descriptions</title>
      <statement>
        <aside>
          <title>Revised in 2026</title>
          <p>In 2025, this question had a part (b) on expressing the position and velocity in polar
            coordinates. It has been removed for simplicity; polar coordinates will be introduced
            later in the course.</p>
        </aside>

        <p> A velocity field is described in Eulerian terms in Cartesian coordinates by <m>
          \mathbf{u}=(-y,x)</m>. What is the Lagrangian position of the particle that starts at the
          point <m>(x_0,y_0)</m>? Describe its path. What is its velocity? </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
          <p> A particle moves with the velocity of the fluid. Then it must satisfy the equations <me>
            \dd{x}{t} = -y(t),\quad \dd{y}{t} = x(t)
            \quad\Rightarrow\quad
            \dd{^2x}{t^2} = -\dd{y}{t} = -x(t).
          </me>
          Trying solutions of the form <m>x(t)=\e^{\lambda t}</m> gives <m>\lambda=\pm i</m>,
          so the general solution is
          <me>
            x(t) = Ae^{it}+Be^{-it},
          </me>
          or, equivalently,
          <me>
            x(t) = C\cos t + D\sin t,
          </me>
          where <m>C</m> and <m>D</m> are constants, and
          <me>
            y(t) =-\dd{x}{t}= C\sin t - D\cos t.
          </me>
          Setting <m>x(0)=x_0</m> and <m>y(0)=y_0</m> gives <m>C=x_0</m> and <m>D=-y_0</m>, so
          <me>
            \bx(t) = (x_0\cos t - y_0\sin t, x_0\sin t + y_0\cos t).
          </me>
          This is a circular path of radius <m>\sqrt{x_0^2+y_0^2}</m> going anticlockwise around the origin.
          The velocity is
          <me>
            \bu(t) = (-x_0\sin t - y_0\cos t, x_0\cos t - y_0\sin t).
          </me>
              </p>
          <p>
            This flow is an example of <em>rigid-body</em> rotation,
            that is the fluid is moving with a velocity field that would be attainable
            for a rigid body, and there is no <em>relative</em> motion of the fluid particles.
          </p>
      </solution>

      <!-- Part (b) on polar coordinates, removed in 2026 for simplicity.
           (The original part (a) was wrapped in introduction and task elements; it is now the
           whole question above.)
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Express the position and velocity in polar coordinates. What do you notice?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
          <p>
            Note that
            <me>
              \hat{\boldsymbol{r}}=
              \left(\begin{matrix}
                \cos\theta\\\sin\theta
              \end{matrix}\right)
              =\cos\theta\boldsymbol{i}+\sin\theta\boldsymbol{j},
              \quad
              \hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}=
              \left(\begin{matrix}
                -\sin\theta\\\cos\theta
              \end{matrix}\right)
              =-\sin\theta\boldsymbol{i}+\cos\theta\boldsymbol{j},
            </me>
            see also <xref ref="eq-polarcartunitvectors-r"/>,
            <xref ref="eq-polarcartunitvectors-theta"/> and
            <xref ref="eq-polarcartunitvectors-z"/>.
          </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
          <p>The calculation works out more simply in polar coordinates; the velocity field is <me>
            \bu = r\be_\theta,
          </me>
          where <m>\be_{\theta}</m> is the angular unit vector given in <xref ref="eq-polarcartunitvectors-theta"/>. Then the equation for particle paths is
          <me>
            \dd{r}{t} = 0,\quad\dd{r\theta}{t}= r\quad\Rightarrow\quad \dd{\theta}{t}=1.
          </me>
          Thus <m>r(t)=r_0=\sqrt{x_0^2+y_0^2}</m> and <m>\theta(t)=\theta_0+t</m>,
          where <m>\theta_0=\tan^{-1}(y_0/x_0)</m>.
          The velocity is <m>\bu(t)=r_0\be_{\theta}(t)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Note that the expression for the velocity in polar coordinates is simpler than
          that in Cartesian coordinates, being in one coordinate direction only.
          Performing this conversion makes it easier to find pathlines and streamlines.
        </p>
        <p>
          This flow is an example of <em>rigid-body</em> rotation,
          that is the fluid is moving with a velocity field that would be attainable
          for a rigid body, and there is no <em>relative</em> motion of the fluid particles.
        </p>
      </solution>
          </task>
      -->
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-hw-eulerian-lagrangian2">
      <title>Eulerian and Lagrangian descriptions</title>
      <introduction>
        <p> A fluid flows through the nozzle shown from <m>x=0</m> to <m>x=L</m> with
          one-dimensional velocity <me>
            u=U\left(1+\frac{x}{L}\right)
          </me> in the <m>x</m>-direction,
          where <m>
            U</m> and <m>L</m> are constants. Note that, in reality the flow would be two- or
          three-dimensional, but we ignore this here for simplicity. </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-nozzle">
          <caption>Sketch of nozzle.</caption>
          <image source="ch-chapter02-nozzle.png" width="60%">
          </image>
        </figure>
      </introduction>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            What is the particle acceleration?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <md>
              <mrow> \ba=&amp;\DD{\bu}{t} </mrow>
              <mrow> =&amp; \pd{\bu}{t} + (\bu \cdot \nabla) \bu</mrow>
              <mrow> =&amp;0+\left(u\pd{u}{x}+v\pd{u}{y}+w\pd{u}{z}\right)\be_x
                +\left(u\pd{v}{x}+v\pd{v}{y}+w\pd{v}{z}\right)\be_y
                +\left(u\pd{w}{x}+v\pd{w}{y}+w\pd{w}{z}\right)\be_z</mrow>
              <mrow> =&amp; u\pd{u}{x}\be_x</mrow>
              <mrow> = &amp; U\left(1+\frac{x}{L}\right)\frac{U}{L}\be_x</mrow>
              <mrow> = &amp; \frac{U^2}{L}\left(1+\frac{x}{L}\right)\be_x</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p> If a particle starts at <m>x=0</m> at time <m>t=0</m>, what is its position at time <m>
            t</m>? </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p> We have <me>
              \dd{x}{t}=u(x)=U\left(1+\frac{x}{L}\right).
            </me> Solving by
            separation of variables gives, <md>
              <mrow>&amp;\int_0^x\frac{dx}{1+x/L}=\int_0^t U\,dt</mrow>
              <mrow>\Rightarrow\quad&amp; L\ln\left(1+\frac{x}{L}\right)=Ut</mrow>
              <mrow>\Rightarrow\quad&amp; x(t)=L\left(\e^{Ut/L}-1\right).</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            What is its Lagrangian velocity as a function of time?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p> The velocity is <md>
              <mrow>\dd{x}{t}=&amp;\dd{}{t}\left(L\left(\e^{Ut/L}-1\right)\right)</mrow>
              <mrow>=&amp; L\frac{U}{L}\e^{Ut/L}</mrow>
              <mrow>=&amp; U\e^{Ut/L}.</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            What is its Lagrangian acceleration as a function of time?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p> The acceleration is <md>
              <mrow>\dd{^2x}{t^2}=&amp;\dd{}{t}\left(U\e^{Ut/L}\right)</mrow>
              <mrow>=&amp; \frac{U^2}{L}\e^{Ut/L}.</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p> Write the Lagrangian velocity and acceleration as a function of <m>x</m>. Compare with
            the corresponding Eulerian velocity and particle acceleration. What do you notice? </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p> The velocity is <me>
              U\e^{Ut/L}=U\left(1+\frac{x}{L}\right),
            </me> as expected.
            This is the same as the Eulerian velocity at the particle position. The acceleration is <md>
              <mrow>\frac{U^2}{L}\e^{Ut/L}=&amp;\frac{U^2}{L}\left(1+\frac{x}{L}\right),</mrow>
            </md>
            which is the same as the particle acceleration, as expected from the definition. </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p> How long does it take for a particle to travel from <m>x=0</m> to <m>x=L</m>? </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p> The particle reaches <m>x=L</m> when <md>
              <mrow>&amp; L\left(\e^{Ut/L}-1\right)=L</mrow>
              <mrow>\Rightarrow\quad&amp; \e^{Ut/L}=2</mrow>
              <mrow>\Rightarrow\quad&amp; t=\frac{L}{U}\ln(2).</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-hw-particle-paths-streamlines">
      <title>Particle paths and streamlines</title>
      <introduction>
        <aside>
          <title>Revised in 2026</title>
          <p>Parts (b) and (c) of this question, together with their hints and solutions, were
            cleaned up for 2026 to set up the pathline and streamline more carefully.</p>
        </aside>
      </introduction>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p> Define the particle paths and streamlines for a velocity field <m>\bu(\bx,t)</m>. When
            do these coincide? </p>
        </statement>


        <solution>
          <p> Particle paths are the trajectories of individual fluid particles, which are found by
            solving the ODE <me>
              \dd{\bx}{t} = \bu(\bx,t).
            </me> Streamlines are curves that
            are instantaneously tangent to the velocity field, and therefore satisfy <me>
              \dd{\bx}{s} = \bu(\bx,t),
            </me> where <m>s</m> is a parameter
            along the streamline. The two coincide if the flow is steady, i.e. <m>
            \bu(\bx,t)=\bu(\bx)</m>. </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p> Let <m>\bx = \bx(t)</m> be a pathline of the fluid, so that <m>\dd{\bx}{t} =
            \bu(\bx(t), t)</m> (see <xref ref="def-particle-path" />), and consider <m>f</m>
            evaluated along the pathline, <me>
              F(t) = f(\bx(t), t),
            </me> which is the value of <m>f</m> experienced by the fluid particle. By drawing a
            sketch of the pathline and marking the positions <m>\bx</m> of the particle at time <m>
            t</m> and <m>\bx + \de\bx</m> at time <m>t + \de t</m>, show that <me>
              \dd{F}{t} = \pd{f}{t} + \bu \cdot \nabla f = \DD{f}{t}.
            </me> Hence show that <m>f</m> is preserved following the flow if and only if <m>
            \DD{f}{t} = 0</m>. </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
          <p> Since <m>\bx(t)</m> is a pathline, the displacement of the particle in a small time <m>
            \de t</m> is <me>
              \de\bx = \bu(\bx, t) \, \de t + O(\de t^2).
            </me> By definition of the derivative, <me>
              \dd{F}{t} = \lim_{\de t \to 0} \frac{f(\bx + \de\bx, t + \de t) - f(\bx, t)}{\de t}.
            </me> To expand the numerator, use the Taylor series for a function of several
            variables: <me>
              f(\bx + \de\bx, t + \de t) = f(\bx, t) + \de\bx \cdot \nabla f(\bx, t) + \de t \,
              \pd{f}{t}(\bx, t) + O\big(|\de\bx|^2, \, |\de\bx| \, \de t, \, \de t^2\big),
            </me> where, in components, <m>\de\bx \cdot \nabla f = \de x \, \pd{f}{x} + \de y \,
            \pd{f}{y} + \de z \, \pd{f}{z}</m>. Substitute for <m>\de\bx</m> and let <m>\de t \to
            0</m>. </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
          <figure xml:id="lagrange-problem">
            <caption>Pathline, showing the particle positions at times <m>t</m> and <m>t+\de t</m>.</caption>
            <image source="ch-chapter02-lagrange-problem2.jpg" width="80%" />
          </figure>

          <p>
            <xref ref="lagrange-problem" /> shows the particle at <m>\bx = \bx(t)</m> and at <m>
            \bx(t + \de t) = \bx + \de\bx</m>. Since the curve is a pathline, <m>\de\bx = \bu(\bx,
            t) \, \de t + O(\de t^2)</m>; in particular, <m>|\de\bx| = O(\de t)</m>, so all of the
            remainder terms in the Taylor series are <m>O(\de t^2)</m>. Following the value of <m>
            f</m> along the pathline, <md>
              <mrow>\dd{F}{t} &amp;= \lim_{\de t \to 0} \frac{1}{\de t} \Big( f(\bx + \de\bx, t + \de
            t) - f(\bx, t) \Big)</mrow>
              <mrow>&amp;= \lim_{\de t \to 0} \frac{1}{\de t} \Big( \de\bx \cdot \nabla f(\bx, t) +
            \de t \, \pd{f}{t}(\bx, t) + O(\de t^2) \Big)</mrow>
              <mrow>&amp;= \lim_{\de t \to 0} \frac{1}{\de t} \Big( \de t \, \bu(\bx, t) \cdot \nabla
            f(\bx, t) + \de t \, \pd{f}{t}(\bx, t) + O(\de t^2) \Big)</mrow>
              <mrow>&amp;= \pd{f}{t} + \bu \cdot \nabla f = \DD{f}{t}.</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>

          <p> The value of <m>f</m> carried by the particle, <m>F(t)</m>, is constant for all <m>
            t</m> if and only if <m>\dd{F}{t} = 0</m>. Since this holds for every pathline, <m>f</m>
            is preserved following the flow if and only if <m>\DD{f}{t} = 0</m>. </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p> Let <m>\bx = \bx(s, t)</m> be a streamline of the flow at fixed time <m>t</m>, so that
            <m>\pd{\bx}{s} = \bu(\bx(s, t), t)</m>, where <m>s</m> is the parameter along the
            streamline (see <xref ref="def-streamline" />). Consider <m>f</m> evaluated on the
            streamline, <me>
              G(s) = f(\bx(s, t), t).
            </me> Show that <m>f</m> is constant along the streamline if and only if <me>
              \bu \cdot \nabla f = 0.
            </me>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
          <p> Consider the points <m>\bx = \bx(s, t)</m> and <m>\bx + \de\bx = \bx(s + \de s, t)</m>
            on the same streamline, at the <em>same</em> instant <m>t</m>. Since the curve is a
            streamline, <me>
              \de\bx = \bu(\bx, t) \, \de s + O(\de s^2).
            </me> By definition of the derivative, <me>
              \dd{G}{s} = \lim_{\de s \to 0} \frac{f(\bx + \de\bx, t) - f(\bx, t)}{\de s}.
            </me> Because the time is the same at both points, the Taylor series now has no
            time term: <me>
              f(\bx + \de\bx, t) = f(\bx, t) + \de\bx \cdot \nabla f(\bx, t) + O(|\de\bx|^2).
            </me>
          </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
          <p> The two points <m>\bx = \bx(s, t)</m> and <m>\bx + \de\bx = \bx(s + \de s, t)</m> lie
            on the same streamline at the same time, and could be sketched as in <xref
              ref="lagrange-problem" />, with the parameter <m>s</m> in place of <m>t</m>. Since the
            curve is a streamline, <m>\de\bx = \bu(\bx, t) \, \de s + O(\de s^2)</m>, so <m>|\de\bx|
            = O(\de s)</m>. Following the value of <m>f</m> along the streamline, <md>
              <mrow>\dd{G}{s} &amp;= \lim_{\de s \to 0} \frac{1}{\de s} \Big( f(\bx + \de\bx, t) -
            f(\bx, t) \Big)</mrow>
              <mrow>&amp;= \lim_{\de s \to 0} \frac{1}{\de s} \Big( \de\bx \cdot \nabla f(\bx, t) +
            O(\de s^2) \Big)</mrow>
              <mrow>&amp;= \lim_{\de s \to 0} \frac{1}{\de s} \Big( \de s \, \bu(\bx, t) \cdot \nabla
            f(\bx, t) + O(\de s^2) \Big)</mrow>
              <mrow>&amp;= \bu \cdot \nabla f.</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>

          <p> Hence <m>G(s)</m> is constant along the streamline if and only if <m>\bu \cdot \nabla
            f = 0</m>. Compared with the previous part, time is frozen here, so there is no <m>
            \pd{f}{t}</m> term. If both the flow and <m>f</m> are steady, then the streamlines and
            pathlines coincide (see the first part) and <m>\DD{f}{t} = \bu \cdot \nabla f</m>, so
            the two conditions are the same. </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-hw-streamlines-pathlines-streaklines">
      <title>Streamlines, pathlines and streaklines</title>


      <introduction>
        <p> The velocity of a two-dimensional fluid flow in Cartesian coordinates is <m>u = 1</m>, <m>v
          = t e^{-4x}</m>. Calculate and sketch the following: </p>
      </introduction>


      <task>
        <statement>
          <p> the streamlines at a fixed time <m>t &gt; 0</m>, </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p> The streamlines are given by <me>
              \pd{y}{x}=te^{-4x}\quad\Rightarrow\quad
              y=y_0-\frac{t}{4} e^{-4x},
            </me>
            for any constant <m>y_0</m>. Different values of <m>y_0</m> give different streamlines.
            See <xref ref="flowvisualisationproblem" /> for a sketch. </p>

          <figure xml:id="flowvisualisationproblem">
            <caption>Sketch of streamlines, pathline and streaklines.</caption>
            <image source="ch-chapter02-flowvisualisationproblem.png" width="100%" />
          </figure>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task>
        <title>This part added in 2026, following marking homeworks.</title>
        <statement>
          <p>Show that the position at time <m>t</m> of the particle that starts from
            the point <m>(x_0,y_0)</m>,</p> is
            <me>
                x = x_0 + t,\quad
                y = y_0 + \frac{1}{16} e^{-4x_0} - \frac{1+4t}{16} e^{-4(x_0+t)}.
            </me>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p> The position of the particle is found by solving
            <me>
              \dd{x}{t}=1,\quad\dd{y}{t}=te^{-4x}
            </me>
            Solving the first equation,
            <me>
              x = A + t,
            </me>
            where <m>A</m> is a constant of integration, and,
            imposing <m>x=x_0</m> at <m>t=0</m>,
            <me>
              x = x_0 + t.
            </me>
            Solving the second equation,
            <me>
              y = \int te^{-4(x_0+t)}\de t
              = B-\frac14 te^{-4(x_0+t)} + \frac14\int e^{-4(x_0+t)}\de t
              = B-\frac14 te^{-4(x_0+t)} - \frac1{16}e^{-4(x_0+t)}.
            </me>
            Substituting the initial condition,
            <me>
              y = y_0 + \frac{1}{16} e^{-4x_0} - \frac{1+4t}{16} e^{-4(x_0+t)}.
            </me>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p> Find the pathline of the particle starting from <m>(x_0,y_0)</m>.
            Hence find and sketch the pathline of the particle that starts from <m>(0,0)</m>, </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            Eliminating <m>t</m> from the particle position found in the previous part:
            <me>
              y = y_0 + \frac{1}{16} e^{-4x_0}
              - \frac{1+4x-4x_0}{16} e^{-4x}.
            </me>
            This is a general pathline. For the particular particle that starts from <m>(0,0)</m>: <me>
            y = \tfrac{1}{16}\left(1 - (1+4x)e^{-4x}\right).
            </me> See <xref
              ref="flowvisualisationproblem" /> for a sketch. </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p> and the streaklines passing through <m>(0,0)</m>. </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
          <p>
            The expression for a general pathline starting from <m>(x_0,y_0)</m> is given by
              <me>
                x = x_0 + t,\quad
                y = y_0 + \frac{1}{16} e^{-4x_0} - \frac{1+4t}{16} e^{-4(x_0+t)}.
              </me>
          </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
          <p> Suppose the particle that started at <m>(x_0,y_0)</m> hits the special point <m>(0,0)</m>
            at time <m>t_1</m>. From the pathline equation: <me>
              0 = x_0 + t_1, \quad
              0 = y_0 + \tfrac{1}{16} e^{-4x_0}
              - \tfrac{1+4t_1}{16} e^{-4(x_0+t_1)}.
            </me>
            Solving for <m>x_0</m> and <m>y_0</m>: <me>
              x_0 = -t_1,\quad
              y_0 = -\tfrac{1}{16}\left(e^{4t_1} - 1 - 4t_1\right).
            </me>
            At time <m>t</m> the particle is at: <me>
              x = t - t_1,\quad
              y = \tfrac{1}{16}\left((1+4t_1) - (1+4t)e^{-4(t-t_1)}\right).
            </me>
            Eliminating <m>t_1</m> gives the streakline at time <m>t</m>: <me>
              y = \tfrac{1}{16}\left((1+4t)(1-e^{-4x}) - 4x\right).
            </me>
            See <xref
              ref="flowvisualisationproblem" /> for a sketch. Note that the streamlines, pathlines
            and streaklines are all very different. </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>For a line/curve in the <m>(x,y)</m>, its timeline at time <m>t</m> is the locus of the
            material elements at time <m>t</m> that started on the line/curve at <m>t=0</m>. In
            experiments this can be visualised using a streak of dye placed initally along the
            line/curve. Tracking this over time gives the timelines.</p>

          <p>Calculate and sketch the timelines of the line (i) <m>y=0</m>, (ii) <m>x=0</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p> From the pathline equation, a particle starting from <m>(x_0,0)</m> reaches <me>
              x = x_0 + t,\quad
              y = \tfrac{1}{16} e^{-4x_0}
              - \tfrac{1+4t}{16} e^{-4(x_0+t)}
            </me>
            at time <m>t</m>. Eliminating <m>x_0</m> gives the timeline: <me>
              y = \tfrac{1}{16}\left(e^{4t} - 1 - 4t\right)e^{-4x}.
            </me>
            See <xref
              ref="flowvisualisationproblem" /> for a sketch. </p>
          <p> From the pathline equation, a particle starting from <m>(0,y_0)</m> reaches <me>
              x = t,\quad
              y = y_0 + \tfrac{1}{16}
              - \tfrac{1+4t}{16} e^{-4t}
            </me>
            at time <m>t</m>. Since <m>y_0</m> can take any value, the timeline is just the line <m>x
            = t</m>. See <xref ref="flowvisualisationproblem" /> for a sketch. </p>
        </solution>
      </task>
    </exercise>


    <exercise xml:id="ex-video-radioactive">
      <title>An experiment for the material derivative</title>

      <introduction>
        <aside>
          <title>Optional</title>
          <p>This question is optional. It is meant only to deepen your understanding of the
            processes discussed in this chapter.</p>
        </aside>

        <p>
          <url
            href="https://techtv.mit.edu/collections/ifluids/videos/32597-eulerian-and-lagrangian-descriptions-in-fluid-mechanics">
          Watch around 12:00 to around 19:00 of the video "Eulerian and Lagrangian Descriptions of
          Fluid Mechanics" from the NCFMF archives</url>. </p>
      </introduction>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>In the video, the authors describe an experiment that one can setup to measure decay of
            a substance, say <m>C(x, t)</m> along a 1D segment in a river.</p>

          <p> In the first situation, it is assumed that <m>C</m> is distributed uniformly in the
            river, but is a naturally decaying substance with some uniform rate of change, <m>
            \dd{C}{t}</m>. Explain what is the (material) change in <m>C</m> that would be measured
            via two sensors, one upstream and one downstream. </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>Next, the authors imagine a situation where <m>C</m> is not uniformly distributed. They
            give a visual and analytical explanation of the material derivative, <me>
              \DD{C}{t} = \pd{C}{t} + u \pd{C}{x},
            </me> where <m>u</m> is
            the horizontal velocity within the infinitesimal 1D section. Write your own explanation
            of the above. </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>


  </exercises>

</chapter>
